Welcome to the Dopamine Guardian, a micro‑agent from the Hyperfocus
Zone’s Agent Army designed to protect your energy levels and fuel the
celebrations that make all the hard work worthwhile. This document explains
how to set up, run and extend the guardian in your own workspace.

Purpose
The Dopamine Guardian is responsible for detecting burnout, boredom and
legendary wins among members of your Discord server. It gathers mood
check‑ins, monitors an optional log stream for significant events and awards
BROski$ tokens when appropriate. When triggered, it sends uplifting
messages, emoji showers and GIFs right into your chosen channel.

Features
Discord bot integration – fully interactive slash commands allow users
to record their mood (/checkin), log wins (/win) and view their
progress (/status).

WebSocket log listener – optionally connect to an external log
endpoint to watch for burnout/boredom/win events from other agents. If a
log event references a specific user the guardian will react directly.

Automatic burnout/boredom detection – a scheduled task runs every
two hours and checks each user’s last check‑in. If no mood has been
recorded in 48 hours or a mood of 3 or less was recorded, the guardian
sends a gentle nudge with self‑care suggestions.

Legendary win celebrations – when a user records a win via /win or
an external log event triggers a win, the guardian awards BROski$ tokens
and posts a celebration message complete with a random GIF.

SQLite memory crystal – all user data, moods, wins and token
balances are persisted in a lightweight SQLite database. You can copy
or backup the database at any time without affecting the running bot.

Graceful degradation – if discord.py is not installed or
environment variables are missing the guardian still runs in console mode
and prints nudges/celebrations, enabling local testing without a Discord
account.

Quick start
Install Python 3.9+ and the required libraries:

bash
Copy
Edit
pip install discord.py websockets aiosqlite
Create or reuse a Discord application:

Visit https://discord.com/developers/applications and create a new
application.

Add a Bot to your application and copy its token.

Enable the MESSAGE CONTENT intent under Privileged Gateway Intents.

Note your guild (server) ID and ensure the bot is invited to that guild.

Set the required environment variables. You can export them in your
shell or create a .env file:

bash
Copy
Edit
export DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN="YOUR_BOT_TOKEN"
export DISCORD_GUILD_ID="123456789012345678"
export DISCORD_CHANNEL_NAME="celebrations"  # optional, defaults to "general"
export LOGS_WEBSOCKET_URL="ws://localhost:8765/logs"  # optional
export REWARD_AMOUNT="15"  # optional, default is 10
Run the guardian:

bash
Copy
Edit
python -m AI.AGENT_DOPAMINE
The guardian will log into Discord and register its slash commands. If
LOGS_WEBSOCKET_URL is set it will also attempt to connect to that
endpoint and listen for JSON log messages.

Slash commands
Command	Description
/checkin	Record your current mood on a scale of 1–10. Mood
recordings update your last active timestamp and help the guardian detect	
burnout/boredom. When a mood of 3 or below is recorded the guardian will	
immediately send a supportive nudge.	
/win	Log a legendary win or achievement. Provide a description
of what you accomplished and the guardian will instantly celebrate your	
success and award BROski$ tokens.	
/status	View your most recent mood and current BROski$ balance.

All commands are available only inside the guild configured by
DISCORD_GUILD_ID. Responses to /checkin and /status are sent as
ephemeral messages (visible only to the invoking user) so moods and balances
remain private.

Log event format
If you configure LOGS_WEBSOCKET_URL the guardian will attempt to parse
incoming messages as JSON objects. The expected structure is:

json
Copy
Edit
{
  "event": "burnout" | "boredom" | "win",
  "discord_id": "123456789012345678",
  "extra": { /* arbitrary data */ }
}
When a burnout or boredom event is received the guardian sends a nudge.
When a win event is received the guardian triggers a celebration. If the
message cannot be parsed as JSON the guardian falls back to a keyword search
(words like burnout, boredom, legendary win), prints a log message and
takes no further action.

Dopamine‑optimised workflow
This module fits into the larger Hyperfocus Zone ecosystem as a
motivation and feedback loop. Below is a suggested workflow for your team:

Daily check‑ins: Encourage every member to run /checkin at the
start and end of their focus sessions. This regular cadence ensures the
guardian has up‑to‑date data to detect dips in mood or energy.

Celebrate everything: Wins—no matter how small—should be logged via
/win. Frequent celebrations not only award BROski$ tokens but also
create a positive reinforcement loop in the server. The guardian
automatically handles the gamification; you just have to record the
achievement.

Integrate with your pipelines: Other agents or services in your
Hyperfocus stack can emit WebSocket log events to LOGS_WEBSOCKET_URL.
For example, your CI pipeline could send a win event when tests pass,
or your Pomodoro timer could send a burnout event if a user cancels
multiple sessions in a row. This turns the guardian into a central
celebration hub.

Review and iterate: The SQLite database stores mood and win
histories. Use this data to identify patterns—are moods dipping at
certain times? Are some team members not logging wins? Adjust your
rituals and scheduling accordingly.

Extend and customise: The guardian is intentionally simple and
modular. Feel free to adjust the REWARD_AMOUNT, update the list of
GIFs in AGENT_DOPAMINE.py, or hook additional commands into the bot.
The community thrives on contributions!

Security considerations
The guardian stores Discord IDs and mood information in an SQLite database.
While no sensitive personal data is captured, you should still treat the
database with care. If you enable WebSocket log listening ensure that the
endpoint is authenticated and encrypted if it carries user‑specific
information. Never hard‑code secrets (tokens, DB paths) in the source;
always rely on environment variables as shown above.

Troubleshooting
Bot not responding to commands: Ensure the bot has been invited with
the correct permissions and that DISCORD_GUILD_ID matches your server.
Check the console for any startup errors.

No celebrations appear: Confirm that REWARD_AMOUNT is set and that
your mood/win commands are executed. Inspect the database to verify
records are being created. Also ensure that your DISCORD_CHANNEL_NAME
matches an existing channel.

WebSocket errors: If the log listener prints repeated connection
errors, verify the URL, network connectivity and format of emitted
messages. The guardian will retry automatically with a short delay.

